<p>
Over at <a href="https://ray-on-emacs.blogspot.com">Ray on Emacs</a>, Ray Zeitler writes about <a href="https://ray-on-emacs.blogspot.com/2026/11/emacs-windows-management-with-windmove.html">moving among windows<br />
and discovering windmove</a>. It was a nostalgic moment for me because<br />
I’ve been in the same place. The problem with <code>other-window</code><br />
(<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <kbd>o</kbd>) is that you may have to invoke it several times and, as Zeitler says, you can easily overshoot your target.
</p>
<p>
The next step in my journey—and apparently in Zeitler’s—was the discovery of <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Window-Convenience.html">Windmove</a>. It allows you to use the arrow keys with (by default) the <kbd>Shift</kbd> key to move through the windows in a two-dimensional way. It’s a big improvement over the standard <code>other-window</code> command but it <i>does</i> interfere with certain Org bindings.
</p>
<p>
The next step in my journey was the discovery of abo-abo’s excellent <a href="https://github.com/abo-abo/ace-window">ace-window package</a>. It allows you to easily move among windows as well as perform many other actions such as splitting, deleting, moving, and copying windows. You can see a complete list at the link. To be honest, I use it almost exclusively for changing focus to another window. It works just like abo-abo’s other ace functions. You invoke it (I use <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <kbd>o</kbd> to leverage my muscle memory) and the windows are labeled. Then you merely choose the label of the window to want to move to. As as added feature, if there are only two windows, it simply moves to the other window without the need for further input. You can also precede the command with the universal argument and it will swap the windows instead of simply moving to the target window.
</p>
<p>
All-in-all, ace-window is the best package I’ve found for window control. Perhaps it will be the next step on Zeitler’s journey too.</p>
